# Target C-II — phase-star orbit capacity gives matched atomic gap `0.0052`

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Repository base:** `c4381ba445ac5b3d1d7a1434b1eeba83ed67708c`  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening of the matched all-train/rhombic atomic gap  
**Scope:** canonical full-core Type-II train-measure branch  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The current hostile-audited iterative-pruning theorem gives the physical two-family
missing-pair inequality

\[
 \rho\ge {2\over29}(a+b-K)_+^2,
 \qquad K={2\over\sqrt3},
\tag{PC.1}
\]

for any two exact train-phase atom families at projective separation `pi/3`, with
`a,b<=K`.  The previous minimax proof feeds (PC.1) into a scalar dominant-orbit
deficit and safely obtains the matched gap `g=0.0050`.

There is a stronger use of the same theorem.  For one exact three-phase orbit, maximize
the **physical crossing mass itself** subject to (PC.1) and the triangle-free
center/leaf correlation.  The resulting orbit-capacity function is strictly below the
generic Mantel capacity `W^2/4`.  Combining that sharpened capacity on the dominant
orbit with the generic square bound on all residual orbit mass gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {T-q/2\over D}
\ge 2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0052,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
}
\tag{PC.2}
\]

The same proof on the strict-rhombus train graph gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {T_R\over D}
\ge 2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0052,
\qquad
\liminf {\sigma_R\over D}\ge0.0104.
}
\tag{PC.3}
\]

The exact rational certificate has more than `1.39e-4` crossing-mass margin at the
claimed endpoint.  A non-load-bearing numerical solve puts the corresponding one-orbit
capacity crossing near `g=0.0053500`; only `0.0052` is claimed here.

---

## 1. Input: unconditional physical pair deficit

Write

\[
\alpha={2\over29}.
\]

The hostile-audited iterative-pruning corollary says that if two exact train-phase atom
families of normalized masses `a,b<=K` have projective axes separated by `pi/3`, and
`rho` is their normalized missing-pair mass, then

\[
\boxed{
\rho\ge\alpha(a+b-K)_+^2.
}
\tag{PC.4}
\]

No lower-size hypothesis is needed.  The common-neighbor and opposite-corner geometry
is internal to the theorem supplying (PC.4).

---

## 2. A physical crossing-capacity theorem for one exact orbit

Fix one exact `pi/3` orbit.  Let its three phase-class masses be ordered

\[
C\ge U\ge V\ge0,
\qquad
W=C+U+V.
\tag{PC.5}
\]

Let `e(C,U)`, `e(C,V)`, and `e(U,V)` be the corresponding limiting crossing masses and
put

\[
x=CU-e(C,U),
\qquad
y=CV-e(C,V).
\tag{PC.6}
\]

If `E` is the total crossing mass of this orbit, define

\[
h:=C(U+V)-E=x+y-e(U,V).
\tag{PC.7}
\]

For every actual `UV` crossing, triangle-freeness makes the two endpoint neighborhoods
in the center class disjoint.  The standard center-degree deficit sum therefore gives

\[
C e(U,V)\le Vx+Uy.
\tag{PC.8}
\]

With

\[
a=1-{V\over C},
\qquad
b=1-{U\over C},
\]

(PC.7)--(PC.8) imply

\[
\boxed{a x+b y\le h.}
\tag{PC.9}
\]

The pair theorem (PC.4) gives

\[
x\ge\alpha(C+U-K)_+^2,
\qquad
y\ge\alpha(C+V-K)_+^2.
\tag{PC.10}
\]

### Lemma PC.1 — equal leaves minimize the forced center/leaf penalty

For fixed `W` and `C`, subject to `U+V=W-C` and `C>=U>=V>=0`,

\[
\boxed{
 a(C+U-K)_+^2+b(C+V-K)_+^2
 \ge
 {3C-W\over C}
 \left({W+C\over2}-K\right)_+^2.
}
\tag{PC.11}
\]

#### Proof

Put

\[
\ell={U+V\over2},
\qquad d={U-V\over2}\ge0,
\qquad r=C+\ell-K,
\qquad A_0=1-{\ell\over C}\ge0.
\]

Then

\[
a=A_0+{d\over C},
\qquad b=A_0-{d\over C}.
\]

If `r<=-d`, both positive parts vanish and (PC.11) is equality.  If
`-d<r<=d`, only the `U` term can be active.  For `r<=0` the right side vanishes; for
`0<r<=d`,

\[
(A_0+d/C)(r+d)^2\ge4A_0r^2\ge2A_0r^2.
\]

If `r>d`, both terms are active and direct expansion gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
&(A_0+d/C)(r+d)^2+(A_0-d/C)(r-d)^2\\
&=2A_0r^2+2A_0d^2+{4rd^2\over C}
\ge2A_0r^2.
\end{aligned}
\]

Finally `2A_0=(3C-W)/C` and `r=(W+C)/2-K`.  This proves (PC.11).  \(\square\)

Combining (PC.9)--(PC.11) yields the one-orbit capacity bound

\[
\boxed{
E\le\Phi(W,C):=
C(W-C)-\alpha{3C-W\over C}
\left({W+C\over2}-K\right)_+^2.
}
\tag{PC.12}
\]

Define

\[
\boxed{
\Psi(W):=\max_{W/3\le C\le W}\Phi(W,C).
}
\tag{PC.13}
\]

Then every exact orbit of total mass `W` has crossing mass at most `Psi(W)`.

---

## 3. Shape of the capacity maximizer in the relevant interval

The endpoint proof only needs

\[
M_0\le W\le A_0,
\tag{PC.14}
\]

where

\[
A_0:=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0052,
\qquad
M_0:={4\Delta_2\over A_0}.
\tag{PC.15}
\]

The exact verifier gives

\[
M_0>1.8994>\sqrt3.
\tag{PC.16}
\]

Hence for `C>=W/3`,

\[
r:={W+C\over2}-K>0.
\]

On `W/3<=C<=W/2`, put

\[
q=3-{W\over C}.
\]

Then `0<=q<=1`, and

\[
\Phi(W,C)=C(W-C)-\alpha q r^2.
\tag{PC.17}
\]

Differentiating in `C`,

\[
\Phi_C
=W-2C-\alpha\left({W\over C^2}r^2+qr\right),
\tag{PC.18}
\]

and

\[
\Phi_{CC}
=-2-\alpha\left[
-{2Wr^2\over C^3}+{2Wr\over C^2}+{q\over2}
\right].
\tag{PC.19}
\]

In this interval `0<r<C`: indeed `r>0` follows from (PC.16), while `r<C` is equivalent
to `W-C<2K`, and `W-C<=2W/3<2K` throughout (PC.14).  Therefore the bracket in
(PC.19) is nonnegative:

\[
-{2Wr^2\over C^3}+{2Wr\over C^2}+{q\over2}
={2Wr\over C^2}\left(1-{r\over C}\right)+{q\over2}
\ge0.
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{\Phi_{CC}\le-2.}
\tag{PC.20}
\]

For `C>=W/2`, the first term in (PC.18) is nonpositive and the penalty derivative is
positive, so `Phi_C<0`.  The verifier also checks uniformly on (PC.14) that
`Phi_C(W,W/3)>0`.  Consequently `Psi(W)` has a unique maximizer

\[
\boxed{C_*(W)\in(W/3,W/2).}
\tag{PC.21}
\]

This is important below because it gives `q<=1` at the optimizer.

---

## 4. Dominant-orbit reduction for the whole atomic measure

Let the exact orbit masses be `M_r`, with total atomic mass

\[
A=\sum_rM_r,
\]

and let

\[
M=\max_rM_r.
\]

The audited crossing decomposition gives

\[
\sum_rE_r=\Delta_2.
\tag{PC.22}
\]

The generic Mantel capacity still gives

\[
4\Delta_2\le\sum_rM_r^2\le MA,
\]

so if `A<=A_0`,

\[
\boxed{M\ge {4\Delta_2\over A}\ge M_0.}
\tag{PC.23}
\]

Apply (PC.13) to one dominant orbit and the generic square capacity to all residual
orbits.  Since the residual masses sum to `A-M`,

\[
\boxed{
\Delta_2
\le\Psi(M)+{(A-M)^2\over4}.
}
\tag{PC.24}
\]

For fixed `M`, the right side is increasing in `A`, so under `A<=A_0` it is enough to
consider `A=A_0`.

It remains to maximize over `M in [M_0,A_0]`.  At the unique maximizer `C_*(M)` of
`Psi`, the envelope derivative is

\[
\Psi'(M)=\Phi_W(M,C_*(M)).
\]

From (PC.17),

\[
\Phi_W
=C+\alpha{r^2\over C}-\alpha q r
\ge C-\alpha q r.
\tag{PC.25}
\]

By (PC.21), `C>=M/3`, `q<=1`, and

\[
r\le {3A_0\over4}-K.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\Psi'(M)
\ge {M_0\over3}
-\alpha\left({3A_0\over4}-K\right).
}
\tag{PC.26}
\]

The exact certificate gives the conservative lower bound

\[
\Psi'(M)>0.6139.
\tag{PC.27}
\]

On the other hand,

\[
{A_0-M\over2}
\le {A_0-M_0\over2}<0.00520.
\]

Hence

\[
{d\over dM}
\left[
\Psi(M)+{(A_0-M)^2\over4}
\right]
>0.6088>0.
\tag{PC.28}
\]

Thus the right side of (PC.24) is largest at

\[
\boxed{M=A_0.}
\tag{PC.29}
\]

The residual atomic mass disappears at the extremum.  The entire atomic-capacity
problem has therefore reduced to the capacity of one physical three-phase orbit:

\[
\Delta_2\le\Psi(A_0).
\tag{PC.30}
\]

---

## 5. Exact rational certificate at `g=0.0052`

The verifier

`research/verify_target_c_phase_star_orbit_capacity_gap_0052.py`

uses exact `Fraction` arithmetic.  It encloses `pi` by Machin's identity

\[
\pi=16\arctan(1/5)-4\arctan(1/239)
\]

with alternating-series remainder bounds, and encloses every square root by integer
square-root arithmetic.

At

\[
c_0=0.94043
\]

it evaluates `Phi(A_0,c_0)` and `Phi_C(A_0,c_0)` with outward rational bounds.  Since
(PC.20) gives `Phi_CC<=-2`, strong concavity implies

\[
\max_C\Phi(A_0,C)
\le
\Phi(A_0,c_0)+{\Phi_C(A_0,c_0)^2\over4}.
\tag{PC.31}
\]

The certificate proves

\[
\boxed{
\Delta_2-\Psi(A_0)
>1.3913\times10^{-4}.
}
\tag{PC.32}
\]

It also checks the derivative facts used above, including

\[
\Phi_C(W,W/3)>0
\]

throughout the entire interval (PC.14), and the monotonicity margin in (PC.28).

Equations (PC.30) and (PC.32) contradict each other.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{A>2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0052-o(1).}
\tag{PC.33}
\]

The exposed-hull train-measure inequality gives `A<=C=(T-q/2)/D`, proving (PC.2).

---

## 6. Rhombic-only transfer

The hostile audit of the earlier matched minimax theorem already checked the same
train-phase framework independently on the strict-rhombus train graph:

- vertices are non-singleton complete rhombic trains;
- the graph is simple and triangle-free;
- its crossing count is exactly `R_4`;
- limiting crossings occur only at exact projective separation `pi/3`; and
- the physical two-family theorem (PC.4) applies to selected center/leaf families.

Nothing in Sections 2--5 uses singleton trains or an all-train-only exposed-hull step.
Repeating the capacity proof on the rhombic graph gives

\[
\liminf {T_R\over D}
\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0052.
\]

The standard exact transfer

\[
\sigma_R=B_4-4\sqrt{R_4},
\qquad B_4=2T_R,
\]

then yields

\[
\boxed{\liminf {\sigma_R\over D}\ge0.0104.}
\]

---

## 7. Frontier effect

This result is not a decimal retuning of the old minimax certificate.  The old route
first compresses the dominant orbit into one scalar deficit and then applies a weighted
two-leaf contradiction.  The present route instead computes a sharp enough upper
capacity for the entire physical three-phase crossing graph and only afterwards sums
across exact orbits.

That distinction matters: the old note identified a non-load-bearing scalar ceiling
near `0.00505782508`, whereas the present theorem safely proves `0.0052` and numerically
remains feasible until roughly `0.0053500`.

The immediately useful downstream substitutions are

```text
C_52 = 2 sqrt(Delta_2) + 0.0052,
(T-q/2)/D >= C_52 - o(1),
T_R/D       >= C_52 - o(1),
sigma_R/D   >= 0.0104 - o(1).
```

These can be inserted into the current Section-171 envelope and the hostile-audited
two-bin/shared outer-energy stack.  They do not themselves construct a same-cardinality
strict sub-`D` source or force an exact unit triangle.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only matched atomic strengthening. C-II and Level I remain open.
